The geography of [[Burawa]] encompasses the physical features, climate, and regional divisions of the [[Republic of Burawa]], the largest country by population in [[Southwestern Kaftia]]. With an area of 934,293 km², Burawa occupies a substantial portion of the southwestern peninsula's southern and central zones, straddling the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]] and extending from the [[Mesogean Sea]] coast in the south to the {{w|tropical savanna}} in the north.

The country's geography is defined by three principal features: the southern coastline, which faces the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] in the west and the [[Mesogean Sea]] in the east, supporting both {{w|humid subtropical climate|humid subtropical}} and {{w|Mediterranean climate|Mediterranean}} climates; the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]], which crosses the country's northern interior and includes [[Buur Waaq]], the highest point in [[Southwestern Kaftia]]; and the [[Lake Haro|Lake Haro System]], a major freshwater complex in the southeast. The [[Buran River]], the longest river within Burawa at 585 km, drains from the highlands to the Mesogean coast and has historically served as the country's principal artery of commerce and settlement.

== Location and boundaries ==

Burawa is situated in the southern portion of [[Southwestern Kaftia]], bounded by the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] to the southwest and the [[Mesogean Sea]] to the southeast, and sharing land borders with eight countries. Proceeding clockwise from the west, these are [[Kijura]], [[Fayeha]], [[West Dakare]], [[Dakare]], [[Kushish]], [[Kwalib]], [[Maianka]], and [[Sabia]].

The country's borders are a mixture of natural and political boundaries. The border with [[Kijura]] is entirely political, while the border with [[Fayeha]] follows the ridge of the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]] until a sharp turn, after which it becomes a political boundary. The border with [[West Dakare]] is political, whereas the border with [[Dakare]] follows the crest of the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]] and the [[Dakare Spur]], a natural {{w|drainage divide|watershed divide}} separating the northern savanna drainage from the southern coastal drainage. The borders with [[Kushish]], [[Kwalib]], [[Maianka]], and [[Sabia]] are all political boundaries, crossing the highlands near [[Buur Waaq]], the [[Lake Haro|Lake Haro System]], and the eastern coastal lowlands.

The northernmost point of Burawa lies in the [[Dimar]] region near the border with [[West Dakare]], while the southernmost and westernmost points are both located in the [[Dafar]] region, on the [[Mesogean Sea]] coast and near the border with [[Kijura]] respectively. The easternmost point lies in the [[Eastern Burawa]] region near the border with [[Sabia]].

== Topography ==

Burawa's terrain can be broadly divided into three zones: the southern coastal lowlands, the central highlands and mountain range, and the northern savanna. The [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]] serves as the principal topographic and climatic divide.

=== Great Kaftian Mountain Range ===
{{main|Great Kaftian Mountain Range}}

The [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]] crosses Burawa's northern interior, running diagonally from southwest to northeast. Within Burawa, the range reaches its highest elevation at [[Buur Waaq]] (2,300 m), the highest point in [[Southwestern Kaftia]], located in the [[Waaq Region]] near the border with [[Kushish]]. The range is ancient and heavily eroded, with rounded peaks and wide passes rather than jagged summits.

The portion of the range within Burawa includes several notable features. [[Buur Waaq]] (2,300 m) is the highest peak, located in the [[Waaq Region]]; its name derives from [[Waaq]], the sky deity in traditional Burawi religion. The [[Dakare Spur]] is a northwestern extension of the range that forms the border with [[Dakare]], with elevations of 800–1,200 m. The [[Kushish Highlands]] constitute the central portion of the range, shared with [[Kushish]] and [[Kwalib]], where the highest elevations occur.

=== Southern coastal lowlands ===

The southern portion of Burawa, facing the [[Mesogean Sea]], consists of coastal plains and river valleys. This zone includes the country's most densely populated areas and its principal cities. The terrain is generally flat to gently rolling, with elevation rarely exceeding 200 m except in the immediate foothills of the mountain range.

The coastal lowlands are dissected by several major rivers flowing south to the Mesogean, including the [[Buran River|Buran]], [[Galbeed River|Galbeed]], [[Dafar River|Dafar]], and [[Hooyal River|Hooyal]]. The river valleys contain deep {{w|alluvium|alluvial}} deposits, producing fertile agricultural land.

=== Central highlands ===

Between the coastal lowlands and the mountain range proper lies a zone of elevated terrain known as the Central Highlands or Harada Sare (High Lakes). This transitional zone, with elevations of 400–1,200 m, encompasses the [[Central Burawa]] and [[Buran River Valley]] regions. The highlands are characterised by a {{w|subtropical highland climate}} and contain seven major lakes, from which the region derives its name.

