The [[Buran River]] is the longest and most significant river in Burawa. Rising in the [[Kushish Highlands]], it flows south through the [[Buran River Valley]] before reaching the [[Mesogean Sea]] at the [[Gacanka Iskhal]] (Bay of Iskhal). The river's name is the origin of the country's name: "Burawa" derives from the [[Ufyen language|Ufyen]] term for "land of the mighty river."

=== Lake Haro System ===
{{main|Lake Haro}}

The [[Lake Haro|Lake Haro System]] is a complex of interconnected lakes in southeastern Burawa, shared with [[Maianka]] and [[Sabia]]. With a total surface area of approximately 8,850 km², it is the second-largest lake system in [[Southwestern Kaftia]] after the [[Great Kaftian Lake]]. The system drains south to the [[Mesogean Sea]] via the [[Haroti River]].

Within Burawa, the Lake Haro System lies primarily in the [[Lake Haro]] cultural region. The lake's shores were the heartland of the ancient [[Kingdom of Ufyen]], and the historic cities of [[Jowhar]], [[Ufyen City]], and [[Badalga-Jir]] are located on or near its waters.

== Coastline ==

Burawa possesses an extensive southern coastline that transitions between two bodies of water. The western portion, from the [[Dafar]] region to approximately the [[Kankadadka-Samara Megalopolis]], faces the open [[Aethiopian Ocean]]. Continuing eastward, the coast gradually enters the [[Mesogean Sea]], a semi-enclosed body of water separating [[Southwestern Kaftia]] from [[Illypnia]]. While geographically continuous, this transition has significant climatic implications: the western sections experience oceanic conditions with moderate temperatures and higher humidity, while the eastern sections exhibit the characteristic hot, dry summers of the {{w|Mediterranean climate}}.

The coastline is characterised by a series of bays, peninsulas, and river mouths.

