=== Peninsulas and capes ===

Several prominent peninsulas extend from the Burawi coastline. [[Gees Galbeedi]] (Western Cape) is a prominent peninsula in the eastern portion of the [[Western Coast]] region, marking the division between the western and central sections of the coast. [[Jasiiradda Mazar]] (Mazar Peninsula) is a historic peninsula in the [[Iskhal Megalopolis]] region, site of the ancient [[Republic of Mazar]] and the modern city of [[Mazar]]. The [[Adad Peninsula]] forms the northern shore of the [[Gacanka Bari]] (Eastern Bay) in the [[Eastern Burawa]] region.

=== Islands ===

Burawa possesses no major offshore islands, but two small islands of note exist within the coastal bays. [[Jasiiradda Hooyal]] (Hooyal Island), at 144 km², is located within the [[Gacanka Hooyal]] and is the largest island off Burawa's coast. [[Jasiiradda Bari]] (Eastern Island), at 92 km², is located within the [[Gacanka Bari]].

== Climate ==

Burawa's climate is determined by its position relative to the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]] and its proximity to the [[Mesogean Sea]]. The country exhibits three principal climate zones, with several {{w|microclimate}}s in specific areas.

=== Oceanic zone ===

The western coastal regions facing the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] experience a {{w|humid subtropical climate}} with oceanic influences. This zone encompasses the [[Dafar]], [[Western Coast]], and [[Kankadadka-Samara Megalopolis]] regions, as well as the southern portions of the [[Dhulsarren]] region.

Summer averages range from 24–30°C, while winter averages range from 14–20°C, more moderate than the Mesogean coast. Annual rainfall totals 700–1,100 mm, more evenly distributed through the year with a winter maximum. Humidity is moderate to high (55–75%), with frequent morning fog and {{w|marine layer}}.

The open ocean exposure produces milder summers, more even rainfall, and higher humidity than the Mesogean littoral. [[Kankadadka]] and the western coast are notably cooler in summer than [[Iskhal]] and the eastern coast.

=== Mediterranean zone ===

The eastern coastal regions facing the [[Mesogean Sea]] experience a {{w|Mediterranean climate}} characterised by warm, dry summers and mild, wet winters. This zone encompasses the [[Central Coast]] (eastern portion), [[Iskhal Megalopolis]], [[Eastern Burawa]], and [[Lake Haro]] regions, as well as the southern portions of the [[Central Burawa]] region.

Summer averages range from 28–34°C, while winter averages range from 12–18°C. Annual rainfall totals 450–700 mm, strongly concentrated in the winter months (October–March). Humidity is low to moderate (40–60%), notably lower in summer.

The semi-enclosed nature of the [[Mesogean Sea]] produces hotter, drier summers than the Aethiopean coast at similar latitudes. The most temperate conditions are found near the [[Lake Haro|Lake Haro System]], where lake-moderated breezes create mild, stable conditions year-round (18–26°C).

=== Subtropical highland zone ===

The central highlands and the mountain slopes experience a {{w|subtropical highland climate}} with cooler temperatures and more even rainfall distribution. This zone encompasses the [[Waaq Region]], [[Buran River Valley]], and the northern portions of the [[Central Burawa]] and [[Dhulsarren]] regions.

Temperatures average 15–22°C year-round, with greater {{w|diurnal temperature variation|diurnal variation}}. Annual rainfall totals 1,000–1,500 mm, distributed more evenly through the year. Humidity is moderate to high (60–80%), with frequent mist and cloud cover at higher elevations.

At the highest elevations in the [[Waaq Region]], approaching the summit of [[Buur Waaq]], conditions approach an {{w|alpine climate}} with occasional frost and the possibility of snow in winter.

=== Tropical savanna zone ===

The far northern [[Dimar]] region, beyond the mountain range, experiences a {{w|tropical savanna climate}} with high temperatures and a distinct wet season. Temperatures average 24–30°C year-round. Annual rainfall totals 1,500–2,000 mm, concentrated in the wet season (April–October). Humidity is high (65–85%), particularly during the wet season.

=== Microclimates ===

Several areas within Burawa exhibit distinctive {{w|microclimate}}s. The shores of [[Lake Haro]] experience lake-effect moderation, with cooler summers and milder winters than the surrounding lowlands. The seven lakes of the [[Central Burawa]] region create localised humidity and morning mists. The highest elevations near [[Buur Waaq]] experience {{w|alpine climate|alpine}} conditions, with temperatures occasionally dropping below freezing.

